WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.625
Welcome to the AI Foundations capstone.
This is not a

2
00:00:05.625 --> 00:00:11.250
contest for the most impressive output. It
is a demonstration

3
00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:16.875
that you can choose a useful low-risk
objective, plan and

4
00:00:16.875 --> 00:00:22.500
execute an AI-assisted workflow, preserve
evidence without expanding privacy risk,

5
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:28.125
verify every acceptance criterion, recover
from failure, and hand the

6
00:00:28.125 --> 00:00:33.750
work to another person. Passing requires
both knowledge and applied

7
00:00:33.750 --> 00:00:39.375
mastery evidence. A polished result
without traceable boundaries and checks

8
00:00:39.375 --> 00:00:45.000
is not complete.

9
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:51.429
Start with an objective brief. Name the
person or group

10
00:00:51.429 --> 00:00:57.857
who needs the result and the decision or
task it

11
00:00:57.857 --> 00:01:04.286
supports. Define one reviewable
deliverable, a deadline or time box,

12
00:01:04.286 --> 00:01:10.714
and acceptance criteria that can produce
direct evidence. List what

13
00:01:10.714 --> 00:01:17.143
is out of scope. Classify the data before
it enters

14
00:01:17.143 --> 00:01:23.571
any AI service or tool. Remove credentials
and personal, confidential,

15
00:01:23.571 --> 00:01:30.000
licensed, or restricted material the task
does not require. Identify

16
00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:36.429
authoritative sources and distinguish read
access, reversible internal changes,

17
00:01:36.429 --> 00:01:42.857
external actions, spending, permission
changes, and destructive effects. State

18
00:01:42.857 --> 00:01:49.286
which actions are prohibited and which
require exact approval. Choose

19
00:01:49.286 --> 00:01:55.714
a task small enough to complete and verify
safely: a

20
00:01:55.714 --> 00:02:02.143
bounded research note, permitted document
transformation, low-risk code correction,

21
00:02:02.143 --> 00:02:08.571
approved data analysis, or paper design of
a tool workflow.

22
00:02:08.571 --> 00:02:15.000
The capstone measures control and
evidence, not operational consequence.

23
00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:20.417
Compare two proposed capstones. The first
says, build an autonomous

24
00:02:20.417 --> 00:02:25.833
system that fixes production incidents. It
has broad authority, real

25
00:02:25.833 --> 00:02:31.250
customer impact, unclear completion, and
dangerous recovery. Reject it. The

26
00:02:31.250 --> 00:02:36.667
second says, use an approved synthetic
incident packet to produce

27
00:02:36.667 --> 00:02:42.083
a one-page triage draft that maps every
claim to the

28
00:02:42.083 --> 00:02:47.500
packet, identifies unknowns, proposes no
production action, and passes a

29
00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:52.917
factuality, accessibility, and escalation
checklist. The deliverable is bounded, the

30
00:02:52.917 --> 00:02:58.333
evidence is available, and execution is
low risk. Its acceptance

31
00:02:58.333 --> 00:03:03.750
criteria can be observed. A later team
could use the

32
00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:09.167
evidence to decide whether to test a
controlled tool workflow,

33
00:03:09.167 --> 00:03:14.583
but the capstone does not borrow that
authority. Good scope

34
00:03:14.583 --> 00:03:20.000
preserves meaningful reasoning while
removing unnecessary consequence.

35
00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:26.429
Build a workflow plan another person can
follow. Separate instructions,

36
00:03:26.429 --> 00:03:32.857
trusted evidence, untrusted input,
constraints, and the output contract.

37
00:03:32.857 --> 00:03:39.286
Identify the provider or tool surface from
task requirements rather

38
00:03:39.286 --> 00:03:45.714
than habit. Record relevant versions and
allowed data. Break the

39
00:03:45.714 --> 00:03:52.143
work into observable steps with an
expected artifact or check

40
00:03:52.143 --> 00:03:58.571
after each step. Add checkpoints before
external, expensive, destructive,

41
00:03:58.571 --> 00:04:05.000
privacy-sensitive, or permission-changing
actions. Define what happens when evidence

42
00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:11.429
conflicts, a required source is
unavailable, structured output fails, a

43
00:04:11.429 --> 00:04:17.857
tool times out, or an acceptance check
fails. State turn,

44
00:04:17.857 --> 00:04:24.286
time, cost, and retry limits. Name the
final stop and

45
00:04:24.286 --> 00:04:30.714
human handoff. Preserve prompts and
material settings, but do not

46
00:04:30.714 --> 00:04:37.143
request or store hidden reasoning. A
reproducible plan exposes inputs,

47
00:04:37.143 --> 00:04:43.571
actions, and decisions; it does not need
access to private

48
00:04:43.571 --> 00:04:50.000
internal model processes.

49
00:04:50.000 --> 00:04:56.000
Draft your capstone objective in one
sentence. Then name the

50
00:04:56.000 --> 00:05:02.000
first condition that must stop the
workflow, the evidence that

51
00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:08.000
reveals it, and the person or recovery
path that receives

52
00:05:08.000 --> 00:05:14.000
control.

53
00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:20.000
Execute in small observable steps and keep
an evidence log.

54
00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:26.000
Record the objective and plan version,
source titles and stable

55
00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:32.000
locations, access dates, permitted input
descriptions or safe digests, material

56
00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:38.000
prompts, tool requests and results, output
versions, timestamps, checkpoints,

57
00:07:38.000 --> 00:07:44.000
approvals, and decisions needed for
reproduction. Link each entry to

58
00:07:44.000 --> 00:07:50.000
an acceptance criterion or recovery need.
Preserve raw or canonical

59
00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:56.000
inputs separately from generated
transformations when policy permits. Do

60
00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:02.000
not store credentials, access tokens,
hidden reasoning, unrestricted private

61
00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:08.000
data, or full content when a safe
reference or digest

62
00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:14.000
is sufficient. If a tool returns untrusted
instructions, keep them

63
00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:20.000
as evidence without allowing them to
rewrite the plan. If

64
00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:26.000
the plan changes, record what changed and
which evidence justified

65
00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:32.000
it. The evidence log should let a reviewer
trace the

66
00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:38.000
result while keeping the privacy footprint
no larger than the

67
00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:44.000
task requires.

68
00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:50.923
Verify every acceptance criterion
independently. Open authoritative sources

69
00:08:50.923 --> 00:08:57.846
and confirm claim scope. Run deterministic
tests or calculations. Inspect

70
00:08:57.846 --> 00:09:04.769
the target artifact or system directly.
Check meaning, accessibility, privacy,

71
00:09:04.769 --> 00:09:11.692
permissions, and prohibited side effects.
Do not ask the same

72
00:09:11.692 --> 00:09:18.615
model whether its work is correct and
count that as

73
00:09:18.615 --> 00:09:25.538
independent evidence. Record pass, fail,
unknown, or not applicable with

74
00:09:25.538 --> 00:09:32.462
the exact supporting artifact. Then
demonstrate recovery. Use a real

75
00:09:32.462 --> 00:09:39.385
failure encountered during safe work, or
deliberately simulate a likely

76
00:09:39.385 --> 00:09:46.308
failure without creating harmful effects.
Preserve the failure, stop condition,

77
00:09:46.308 --> 00:09:53.231
diagnosis, smallest correction, rerun,
neighboring regression checks, rollback or

78
00:09:53.231 --> 00:10:00.154
escalation, and final state. A passing
final version does not

79
00:10:00.154 --> 00:10:07.077
erase the failed attempt. Recovery
evidence shows that you can

80
00:10:07.077 --> 00:10:14.000
detect and control error rather than
merely avoid discussing it.

81
00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:20.000
Checkpoint. A learner submits an accurate
final report, source links,

82
00:10:20.000 --> 00:10:26.000
and a prompt. The task used internal data,
but the

83
00:10:26.000 --> 00:10:32.000
submission has no classification,
redaction record, authority boundary,

84
00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:38.000
recovery evidence, or reviewer handoff.
The learner self-scores ninety percent.

85
00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:44.000
Can the capstone pass, and what must
happen next?

86
00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:48.000
The capstone cannot pass from the final
report alone. Correctness

87
00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:52.000
and source evidence may earn supported
points, but safety, reproducibility,

88
00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:56.000
recovery, and communication require their
named artifacts. A self-score is

89
00:11:56.000 --> 00:12:00.000
not evidence. Return the submission for
the minimum missing records:

90
00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:04.000
permitted-data classification and
redactions, authority and approval

91
00:12:04.000 --> 00:12:08.000
boundaries, verification and recovery
evidence, reviewer-ready handoff,

92
00:12:08.000 --> 00:12:12.000
reflection, and criterion-by-criterion
evidence mapping. Then rescore. If you

93
00:12:12.000 --> 00:12:16.000
accepted ninety percent because the report
was accurate, revise the

94
00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:20.000
rule: applied mastery includes how the
result was produced, checked,

95
00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:24.000
controlled, and transferred—not only how
it reads.

96
00:12:24.000 --> 00:12:30.250
If no natural failure occurs, simulate one
safely and honestly.

97
00:12:30.250 --> 00:12:36.500
Remove one required source from a research
synthesis, corrupt one

98
00:12:36.500 --> 00:12:42.750
field in a copy of structured output, run
a boundary

99
00:12:42.750 --> 00:12:49.000
input against a local test, or model a
tool timeout

100
00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:55.250
on paper without performing an external
write. State that the

101
00:12:55.250 --> 00:13:01.500
failure was simulated. Show how the
workflow detects it, stops

102
00:13:01.500 --> 00:13:07.750
dependent work, preserves evidence,
identifies the responsible layer, and

103
00:13:07.750 --> 00:13:14.000
chooses correction, retry, rollback, or
escalation. Rerun the original criteria

104
00:13:14.000 --> 00:13:20.250
after repair. Do not fabricate a
production incident, manipulate real

105
00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:26.500
learner records, send test messages to
actual people, or create

106
00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:32.750
a failure that needs authority outside the
capstone. The purpose

107
00:13:32.750 --> 00:13:39.000
is to demonstrate recovery competence, not
to manufacture drama.

108
00:13:39.000 --> 00:13:45.429
Package five required artifacts: the
objective and scope brief; prompt

109
00:13:45.429 --> 00:13:51.857
or workflow plan; source, tool, and result
evidence; verification and

110
00:13:51.857 --> 00:13:58.286
recovery record; and reflection with
reviewer-ready handoff. Score five equally

111
00:13:58.286 --> 00:14:04.714
weighted criteria. Correctness asks
whether the deliverable meets its

112
00:14:04.714 --> 00:14:11.143
objective and criteria. Evidence asks
whether claims and decisions map

113
00:14:11.143 --> 00:14:17.571
to authority or observation. Safety asks
whether data, permissions, approvals,

114
00:14:17.571 --> 00:14:24.000
and consequences stayed bounded.
Reproducibility asks whether another

115
00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:30.429
person can repeat the workflow and
understand recovery. Communication asks

116
00:14:30.429 --> 00:14:36.857
whether the handoff separates facts,
generated suggestions, decisions,

117
00:14:36.857 --> 00:14:43.286
uncertainty, limitations, and next steps.
Award points only when the

118
00:14:43.286 --> 00:14:49.714
required evidence exists. Record reviewer
feedback and preserve revisions.

119
00:14:49.714 --> 00:14:56.143
Completion requires at least eighty
percent on the knowledge check

120
00:14:56.143 --> 00:15:02.571
and at least eighty percent on the
capstone rubric. Neither

121
00:15:02.571 --> 00:15:09.000
substitutes for the other.

122
